\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSDistantObject is a concrete subclass of NSProxy that defines proxies for objects in other applications or threads. When an NSDistantObject receives a message, in most cases it forwards the message through its NSConnection object to the real object in another application, supplying the return value to the sender of the message if one is forthcoming, and propagating any exception back to the invoker of the method that raised it.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSDistantObject adds two useful instance methods to those defined by NSProxy. \b connectionForProxy\b0  returns the NSConnection that handles the receiver. \b setProtocolForProxy:\b0  establishes the set of methods that the real object is known to respond to, saving the network traffic required to determine the argument and return types the first time a particular selector is forwarded to the remote proxy.\
\fs14\
\fs28 There are two kinds of NSDistantObject: local proxies and remote proxies. A local proxy is created by an NSConnection the first time an object is sent to another application. It\'27s used by the NSConnection for bookkeeping purposes and should be considered private. The local proxy is transmitted over the network using the NSCoding protocol to create the remote proxy, which is the object that the other application uses. NSDistantObject defines methods for an NSConnection to create instance, but they\'27re intended only for subclasses to override\'d0you should never invoke them directly. Use NSConnection\'27s \b rootProxyForConnectionWithRegisteredName:host:\b0  method, which sets up all the required state for an object-proxy pair.\
\fs14\
